  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-5x-9$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots.",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3$, and $\\pm 9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
